## What the Project is
This project is an automated spreadsheet-based ETL Workflow.
It automatically extracts, transforms, and organizes many different types of part numbers into ascending order.
It then loads the sorted list into one of three reports that can then be printed out.
These reports are used for the planning and moving of hundreds of parts at a time, to differnt locations in the warehouse.

What is this project?
A spreadsheet-based ETL workflow.
Built to take vendor/product data and turn it into standardized, sorted reports.
Why did you build it?
To automate a process that would otherwise require repetitive manual manipulation of spreadsheet data.
To make the process more consistent and less susceptible to human error.
What does ETL mean in this context?
Extract: source data enters the workbook.
Transform: the workbook cleans, standardizes, parses, and sorts the data.
Load: the processed information is placed into usable output/report worksheets.
What does the project demonstrate?
Spreadsheet automation.
Data transformation.
Business-rule implementation.
QA and validation thinking.
Designing a repeatable process rather than solving a problem manually each time.

## The Problem
It would take over an hour to manually cut and paste the data into ascending order, and is repetitive and error-prone.
A repeatable process w sneeded to transform the source data into standardized, usable reports.
Source files could potentially arrive with inconsistent formatting/structure.
This would need to be a minimal interaction solution, due to potential non-technical users being involved.


